::GEO_U04_L01_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A logo is enlarged from a width of 4 cm to 10 cm. What is the scale factor from the original to the image?
{
=A. 2.5#Scale factor compares image length to original length: 10 divided by 4 is 2.5.
~B. 6#This subtracts the lengths instead of comparing them multiplicatively.
~C. 14#This adds the lengths and does not describe scale factor.
~D. 0.4#This is the reverse scale factor from image to original.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Two similar rectangles have corresponding heights 7 and 21. What is the scale factor from the smaller rectangle to the larger rectangle?
{
~A. 14#This subtracts the heights instead of using a multiplier.
=B. 3#The scale factor is 21 divided by 7, which equals 3.
~C. 28#This adds the heights and does not represent similarity scaling.
~D. &frac13;#This is the scale factor from larger to smaller, not smaller to larger.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A scale copy changes a side from 9 units to 6 units. What is the scale factor from the original to the copy?
{
~A. 3#This uses a whole-number multiplier that would enlarge the side.
~B. 15#This adds the side lengths and does not describe scale factor.
=C. 2/3#The copy-to-original ratio is 6 divided by 9, which simplifies to 2/3.
~D. 3/2#This is the reverse scale factor from the copy back to the original.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Figure A has side lengths 5, 8, and 11. Figure B has corresponding side lengths 10, 16, and 22. What evidence supports similarity?
{
~A. Only one side is doubled.#All three corresponding sides are doubled, not just one.
~B. The figures are similar because they are drawn near each other.#Location in a diagram is not evidence of similarity.
~C. The side differences are all equal.#The differences are not all equal, and similarity depends on ratios rather than differences.
=D. All corresponding side ratios are 2.#Each side in Figure B is twice the matching side in Figure A, so the side ratios are equal.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Figure A has side lengths 3, 4, and 6. Figure B has corresponding side lengths 6, 8, and 12. What is the common scale factor?
{
=A. 2#Each corresponding side in Figure B is 2 times the side in Figure A.
~B. 3#A scale factor of 3 would make the sides 9, 12, and 18.
~C. 4#A scale factor of 4 would make the sides 12, 16, and 24.
~D. 1#A scale factor of 1 would mean the corresponding lengths are unchanged.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q06
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A drawing uses a scale factor of 4 from a small triangle to a large triangle. A 6-inch side on the small triangle corresponds to what length on the large triangle?
{
~A. 10 inches#Adding 4 treats the scale factor as an amount to add instead of a multiplier.
=B. 24 inches#Multiply the original side by the scale factor: 6 times 4 equals 24.
~C. 2 inches#Dividing by 4 scales in the opposite direction.
~D. 18 inches#Multiplying by 3 does not use the given scale factor of 4.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q07
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A scale factor of ½ is applied to a figure. What happens to each corresponding side length?
{
~A. Each side length doubles.#Doubling would use a scale factor of 2.
~B. Each side length increases by ½ unit.#Scale factors multiply lengths; they do not add a fixed amount.
=C. Each side length is halved.#A scale factor of ½ multiplies every length by one-half.
~D. Only one side length changes.#A dilation scale factor applies to all corresponding lengths.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q08
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which statement gives mathematical evidence that two figures are similar?
{
~A. The figures appear to have the same shape.#Appearance alone is not mathematical evidence.
~B. One pair of sides has the same length.#One side pair alone does not establish similarity.
~C. The figures are both shaded.#Shading is visual decoration, not similarity evidence.
=D. Corresponding angles match and corresponding side ratios are equal.#Similarity requires equal corresponding angles and proportional corresponding side lengths.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q09
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Two figures have corresponding side ratios 4:6, 6:9, and 8:12. What do these ratios show?
{
=A. The corresponding side lengths are proportional.#Each ratio simplifies to 2:3, so the side lengths are proportional.
~B. The figures are congruent.#Congruent figures would have a scale factor of 1; these ratios show a different common ratio.
~C. Only the first two sides are proportional.#All three listed ratios simplify to the same relationship.
~D. No comparison is possible.#The ratios provide enough information to compare corresponding sides.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q10
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A scale factor from Figure P to Figure Q is 1. What does that mean about corresponding side lengths?
{
~A. They are doubled.#Doubling would require a scale factor of 2.
=B. They are equal in length.#A scale factor of 1 leaves every corresponding side length unchanged.
~C. They are halved.#Halving would require a scale factor of ½.
~D. They cannot be compared.#A scale factor gives a direct comparison of corresponding lengths.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q11
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A rectangle has sides 6 and 10. A similar rectangle has a side corresponding to 6 that measures 18. What side corresponds to 10?
{
~A. 22#Adding 12 repeats the first side difference, but similarity uses multiplication.
~B. 15#This uses a scale factor of 1.5 instead of 3.
=C. 30#The scale factor is 18 divided by 6, or 3; therefore 10 times 3 equals 30.
~D. 28#This does not use the matching scale factor from corresponding sides.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q12
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A model uses scale factor 5 from a drawing to the actual object. A drawing length is 3 cm. What is the actual length?
{
~A. 8 cm#Adding the scale factor does not apply similarity scaling.
~B. 2 cm#Subtracting does not apply a scale factor.
~C. 1.67 cm#Dividing by 5 would scale from actual object back to drawing.
=D. 15 cm#Multiply the drawing length by the scale factor: 3 times 5 equals 15.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q13
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has sides 4, 7, and 9. A scale copy has sides 12, 21, and 27. Are the triangles similar by side ratios?
{
=A. Yes, each corresponding side ratio is 3.#The second triangle has sides 3 times the first triangle, so the side ratios are equal.
~B. No, because the side differences are not equal.#Similarity depends on ratios, not equal differences.
~C. No, because the triangles have different sizes.#Similar figures may have different sizes when side lengths are proportional.
~D. Yes, because only one side ratio is 3.#All corresponding side ratios must be checked, not just one.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q14
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which pair of ratios is proportional?
{
~A. 3/5 and 5/3#These are reciprocals, not equal ratios.
=B. 8/12 and 2/3#8/12 simplifies to 2/3, so the ratios are equal.
~C. 4/9 and 8/12#These simplify to different values.
~D. 7/10 and 10/7#These are reciprocals, not equal ratios.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q15
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A poster is reduced with scale factor 0.25. Which statement is true?
{
~A. Each corresponding length becomes four times as long.#That would use a scale factor of 4.
~B. Only the width changes.#A scale factor affects every corresponding length.
=C. Each corresponding length becomes one-fourth as long.#A scale factor of 0.25 is equivalent to one-fourth.
~D. Lengths increase by 0.25 unit.#Scale factors multiply lengths rather than adding a fixed amount.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q16
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Two figures have corresponding sides 8 and 20. What is the scale factor from the first figure to the second?
{
~A. 12#This subtracts the side lengths instead of comparing them by ratio.
~B. 28#This adds side lengths and does not give a scale factor.
~C. 0.4#This is the reverse scale factor from the second figure to the first.
=D. 2.5#The scale factor is 20 divided by 8, which equals 2.5.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q17
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A similar figure has a side length 45 corresponding to original side length 15. Which scale factor was used?
{
=A. 3#Divide the image length by the original length: 45 divided by 15 equals 3.
~B. 30#This subtracts the lengths instead of using a ratio.
~C. 60#This adds the lengths and does not describe similarity.
~D. &frac13;#This is the reverse scale factor.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q18
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A scale factor of 2.2 is applied to a segment of length 5. What is the image length?
{
~A. 7.2#Adding 2.2 treats the scale factor as an addend.
=B. 11#Multiply 5 by 2.2 to get 11.
~C. 2.8#Subtracting the scale factor does not describe dilation.
~D. 2.27#Dividing by 2.2 scales in the opposite direction.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q19
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A student claims two quadrilaterals are similar because both have four sides. What is missing?
{
~A. The color of each figure#Color does not determine similarity.
~B. The area of the page#The page area is unrelated to figure similarity.
=C. Evidence about corresponding angles and side ratios#Having four sides only identifies both as quadrilaterals; similarity requires angle and proportional side evidence.
~D. The names of the vertices only#Vertex names help with correspondence but do not alone prove similarity.
}


::GEO_U04_L01_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L01_Q20
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Side ratios from Figure A to Figure B are 2, 2, 2, and 2. What conclusion is reasonable if corresponding angles also match?
{
~A. The figures cannot be compared.#The ratios and angle evidence allow a comparison.
~B. The figures must have scale factor 1.#The scale factor shown by the ratios is 2, not 1.
~C. The figures are unrelated because they have four sides.#Four-sided figures can be similar when correspondence evidence is present.
=D. The figures are similar.#Equal corresponding side ratios together with matching angles support similarity.
}

